WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.965
Unsafe Rust is not a vague label.

2
00:00:02.045 --> 00:00:05.192
It is tied to specific categories of
operations that need extra care.

3
00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:08.635
Code may need unsafe to dereference raw
pointers, call unsafe functions,

4
00:00:08.715 --> 00:00:11.916
access or modify mutable static variables,
implement unsafe traits,

5
00:00:11.996 --> 00:00:13.244
or access fields of unions.

6
00:00:13.324 --> 00:00:17.122
Each category asks the programmer to
uphold rules the compiler cannot fully check.

7
00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:20.349
Raw pointer dereferencing requires review
of validity, alignment,

8
00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:22.978
initialization, lifetime, and aliasing
assumptions.

9
00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:25.608
A pointer-like value is not the same as a
Rust reference.

10
00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:28.889
The code must establish that the memory is
live, suitable for the type,

11
00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:31.682
and used according to the rules the
abstraction depends on.

12
00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:35.451
Calling an unsafe function means the
caller must satisfy preconditions that the

13
00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:36.616
compiler cannot check.

14
00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:40.223
The function may require a pointer to be
valid, a buffer length to be accurate,

15
00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:44.101
a value to follow a representation
contract, or a thread-safety condition to hold.

16
00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:47.653
Review should look at both the function
contract and the caller's evidence.

17
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.445
Mutable statics introduce global
shared-state concerns.

18
00:00:50.525 --> 00:00:54.486
Any design that allows process-wide
mutable state needs careful synchronization and

19
00:00:54.566 --> 00:00:55.380
clear ownership.

20
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.095
Unsafe traits are also sensitive because
other code may rely on the implementer

21
00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:00.748
correctly upholding guarantees.

22
00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:04.355
A wrong implementation can break
assumptions far away from where it appears.

23
00:01:04.435 --> 00:01:08.124
Union field access depends on
representation and initialization assumptions.

24
00:01:08.204 --> 00:01:10.916
Reviewers should ask what data is active,
how that is known,

25
00:01:10.996 --> 00:01:13.438
and how the abstraction prevents
accidental misuse.

26
00:01:13.518 --> 00:01:15.797
The practical goal of this module is
recognition:

27
00:01:15.877 --> 00:01:19.620
when you see one of these categories, you
know what kind of safety questions to ask.
